III. ^^Annotations to the Table.^^

Nos. 1 to 11 are those given in the text, pp. 262-5, but No. 8 was merely suggested by Prof. Helmholtz.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
Nos. 12 to 24 are from App. III. to Prof. Land's paper, *Over de Toonladders der Arabische Musiek* (on the Arabic musical scales), and contain his corrections of the very faulty MS. of Al Farabi; the numbers are also given by Kosegarten, p. 55. After 24, the numbers ⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
suddenly cease in the MS.

Nos. 25 to 31 are the old theoretical form of the Greek scales with the old tetrachords, see suprà, p. 268 *c*, *d'*.

Nos. 32 to 38 are taken from suprà, p. 269 *a*.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
Nos. 39 to 50 are from Prof. Land, *ibid.* p. 38, corrected from the MS. at Leyden; for No. 45 the copyist had repeated No. 44, and Prof. Land has supplied the numbers by analogy.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
¶ No. 51 is inferred from the complete set of notes used on the Arabic lute at different times as shewn in No. 66.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
No. 52. The Highland bagpipe representing that scale has been inserted immediately afterwards to shew its practical identity. It was played to Mr. Hipkins and myself by Mr. C. Keene, the artist.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
No. 53. The very modern survival of the same scale has been put next. It is described, suprà, p. 264 note ⁎⁎. In practice each note might be sharpened by one or more quartertones.

⟦A⟧
>>>
<<<
⟦A⟧
===
Nos. 54 to 65 are the twelve scales given, suprà, p. 284, from Prof. Land, but the four which employ 8 notes are now placed last. No. 61, *Hhidjazi*, is, in fact, more harmonic than the usual equal temperament. If we begin on the note vii., and reckon the
